<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balian of Ibelin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1143–<a href="/years/1193">1193</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin">House of Ibelin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balian of Ibelin was a leading baron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> and the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-of-ibelin">Barisan of Ibelin</a>. In 1177 he married the dowager queen Maria Komnene, gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>. After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> he led the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1187 and negotiated its surrender, allowing many inhabitants to ransom themselves. He later helped negotiate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa</a> of 1192.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balian-of-ibelin.jpg" alt="Balian of Ibelin" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archer, Thomas Andrew; Kingsford, Charles Lethbridge · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sceau_de_Balian_d%27Ibelin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Balian was the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-of-ibelin">Barisan of Ibelin</a>, a knight in the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> who had been rewarded with the lordship of Ibelin after the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-ii-of-jaffa">Hugh II of Jaffa</a>, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvis-of-ramla">Helvis of Ramla</a>. He was baptised Barisan and adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> form Balian around 1175–76; Arabic sources call him Balian ibn Barzan. He first appears in charters in 1158. After the death of his eldest brother Hugh, the next brother, Baldwin, preferred to remain lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> and gave Ibelin to Balian, who held it as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Balian supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond III of Tripoli</a> as regent in 1174 and led the vanguard at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montgisard">Battle of Montgisard</a> in 1177. That year he married Maria Komnene, widow of King Amalric I, and received the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>, her dower. In 1186 he and Maria put forward Amalric's daughter Isabella for the throne, but her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-iv-of-toron">Humphrey IV of Toron</a> swore fealty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a>, and Balian reluctantly paid homage to Guy.</p><p>Balian escaped from the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. Saladin allowed him to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to bring out his wife and children, on condition that he swear never to bear arms against him; the patriarch Eraclius absolved him of the oath. Finding the city almost without knights, Balian made 60 burgesses knights and prepared for a siege, which began on 20 September 1187. When the walls could not hold, he negotiated terms: Saladin would free seven thousand men for 30,000 bezants, and Balian handed over the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> on 2 October.</p><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> he and Maria backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a>, whom Isabella married after her first marriage was annulled. This brought them the hatred of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a>'s supporters. In 1192 Balian commanded the rearguard at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and helped negotiate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa</a> between Richard and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Balian died in 1193, in his early fifties. He and Maria had four children, among them John of Ibelin, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, and Philip of Ibelin, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. His squire Ernoul wrote parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> continuation of William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>'s chronicle, with a strong bias in favour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin">Ibelin family</a>. The name Balian remained common in the family through the 13th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balian of Ibelin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shortly after the Battle of Jaffa, Richard I of England and Saladin, Sultan of Egypt, agreed a three-year truce, negotiated with the help of <b>Balian of Ibelin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-2">2 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Balian of Ibelin</b> surrendered the city twelve days later, and most inhabitants were allowed to ransom themselves.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-20">20 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Barisan's sons Hugh, Baldwin and <b>Balian of Ibelin</b> were leading barons; Balian married the dowager queen Maria Komnene.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ibelin">Lordship of Ibelin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1175" aria-label="Holy Land on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Balian of Ibelin"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 4839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 4839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1175">What was here in 1175? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
